## Why it can throw

`DefaultListableBeanFactory.registerBeanDefinition` (spring-beans/.../DefaultListableBeanFactory.java:1252-1276) checks whether a bean name is already registered:

```java
BeanDefinition existingDefinition = this.beanDefinitionMap.get(beanName);
if (existingDefinition != null) {
    if (!isBeanDefinitionOverridable(beanName)) {
        throw new BeanDefinitionOverrideException(beanName, beanDefinition, existingDefinition);
    }
    else {
        logBeanDefinitionOverriding(beanName, beanDefinition, existingDefinition);
    }
    this.beanDefinitionMap.put(beanName, beanDefinition);
}
```

The same guard applies when the new bean name is actually an existing alias pointing at another bean definition (lines 1279-1291).

`isBeanDefinitionOverridable` (line 1447-1449) delegates to `isAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding()` (line 279-281), which is backed by the `allowBeanDefinitionOverriding` flag (default `null`, treated as `true` unless explicitly set to `false` — see the Javadoc at line 264-268: *"Default is 'true'"*).

## When it happens

- Two different `BeanDefinition`s are registered under the *same bean name*, **and**
- the factory's `allowBeanDefinitionOverriding` has been explicitly set to `false` via `setAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding(false)` (line 270-272).

Typical triggers: two `@Configuration` classes (or XML + `@Bean`) defining a bean with the same name, duplicate `@Component`/`@Bean` names from overlapping component scans, or importing a third-party config that happens to reuse a bean name you already defined. `ConfigurationClassBeanDefinitionReader` (spring-context/.../ConfigurationClassBeanDefinitionReader.java:345, 370) also throws this exception when it detects a `@Bean` method colliding with an existing non-overridable definition.

Note Spring Boot sets `allowBeanDefinitionOverriding` to `false` by default (since Boot 2.1), which is why this exception is far more commonly seen in Boot apps than in plain Spring Framework usage (where the framework-level default is `true`).

## How applications commonly avoid it

1. **Avoid the name collision in the first place** — rename one of the conflicting beans, or scope component scanning/`@Import`s so the same bean name isn't produced twice.
2. **Use `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` / `@Primary`** instead of relying on override semantics — the idiomatic Boot pattern for "let the user replace my default bean" without touching the override flag at all.
3. **Explicitly opt in to overriding** when it's intentional: call `((DefaultListableBeanFactory) beanFactory).setAllowBeanDefinitionOverriding(true)`, or in Spring Boot set `spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding=true`.
